# Regression test for docs/known-issues-biguint-spike.md item 3: the stdlib
# `Error` enum (book.md, "Standard error enum") must be resolvable without any
# user declaration, on both `boring run` and `boring build`.
#
# Each case uses its own `try` with a single matching `catch Error.Variant:`
# clause — deliberately not exercising multiple `catch Error.X: / catch
# Error.Y:` clauses on the same `try`, which was a separate, pre-existing
# interpreter bug unrelated to this one (the first catch clause matched
# regardless of variant; reproduced identically with a plain user-defined
# enum, nothing to do with the builtin `Error` type or this fix). Now fixed
# and covered by tests/cases/multi_variant_catch_dispatch.br.
def check_low(int n) throws:
    if n < 0:
        throw Error.InvalidInput
    print "ok {n}"

def check_high(int n) throws:
    if n > 100:
        throw Error.OutOfBounds
    print "ok {n}"

def main() throws:
    try:
        check_low(-1)
    catch Error.InvalidInput:
        print "caught invalid input"

    try:
        check_high(200)
    catch Error.OutOfBounds:
        print "caught out of bounds"

    try:
        check_low(5)
    catch Error.InvalidInput:
        print "caught invalid input"
